boeing's popular 787 dreamliner is  facing a new manufacturing defect  which might sound familiar this is  another blow  for boeing the defect could take three  weeks to address which means airlines  won't be receiving dreamliners in time  for much  of the busy summer travel season recent  issues with the plane makers wide-body  aircraft  stretch back to last summer since then  the defects have prompted regulatory  review  a halting of deliveries a pileup of more  than 100 planes and are now pressuring  boeing's bottom line  this is not a situation boeing wants to  be in at this point  right now it can't deliver its most  popular wide-body aircraft it's almost  as if a company like apple couldn't  deliver  laptops so what are the issues with the  dreamliner  and how did boeing get here first a bit  of background  the 787 dreamliner made its commercial  debut in 2011.  promised all sorts of possibilities for  new routes  new efficiencies for airlines a better  passenger experience  and then it wound up on the ground in  early 2013 a fire broke out aboard an  empty dreamliner in boston  sparked by battery problems that later  surfaced on another dreamliner in japan  in two weeks time we saw two cases  of battery failures on the 787  and the grounding of the entire fleet  by the faa so the dreamliner had to  emerge  from that experience and regain the  confidence  of airlines and the traveling public the  787 is now one of boeing's  most popular aircraft more than 60  airlines including american and united  have added 787s to their fleet the  dreamliner is unique  as it was boeing's first plane designed  largely by other companies  its primary structure is made mostly  from composite materials for efficiency  because the dreamliners structure is new  in the world of aircraft manufacturing  boeing and the faa  are learning how its composite materials  and where those materials join  age over time so fast forward nearly a  decade of flights later to august of  last year when boeing voluntarily  grounded eight dreamliners  found with two different production  defects in the same location  for an aircraft manufacturer to do that  on its own is  something that you really don't see that  often the defects were outlined in an  august 31st  internal federal aviation administration  memo which was reviewed  and then reported on by the wall street  journal we first reported  about the dreamliner issues in september  when  the faa launched a high-level review  of boeing's dreamliner manufacturing  processes  at that time boeing and the faa were  trying to get their heads around the  problem  and they focused on one general area of  the aircraft  specifically the faa memo says they were  looking at non-conforming sections of  the rear fuselage or body of the plane  that fell short of its own engineering  standards the first defect had to do  with how boeing used shims or the  material that fills the gaps between  sections of the fuselage the second  stemmed from variations in the  smoothness of the fuselage skin  while the two types of defects  independent of each other  didn't pose an immediate safety risk  together in the same  section were deemed to potentially  introduce a risk  of a structural integrity problem in  extreme circumstances then the following  day  boeing flagged a third issue this one  with sections of the dreamliner's tail  that were clamped together  improperly during assembly it's not a  question of  immediate structural integrity it's a  question of whether or not they  introduce  premature aging and if so what does that  mean  for the dreamliners in service and how  often  they need to be serviced and fixed in a  recent internal video boeing explained  how thin these gaps are  if you start at the tail of the airplane  just walking to the front  each of those sections when we bring  them together is within  five thousandths of an inch you'll hear  people talk of gaps  it's really not what you would normally  think of as a gap  the next month in october boeing halted  delivery of the dreamliners  halting deliveries is a big deal in  effect that means boeing can't sell the  products  customers largely pay boeing on delivery  prior to the halt many of boeing's  commercial deliveries were dreamliners  as the 737 max remained grounded  following two fatal crashes of that  aircraft  boeing was at that point a month away  from  getting the is okay to again  fly passengers on 737 max and suddenly  it couldn't get  its other popular aircraft the  dreamliner  off the lot either then with delivery  still halted the plane maker expanded  its inspections of the dreamliner in  december  after it found instances of the  smoothness issue on new sections of the  plane  at that point it became clear that  the problem wasn't going away as quickly  as we might have thought earlier in  mid-march the faa took back boeing's  authority to inspect and sign off on  several dreamliners awaiting delivery  saying it would conduct the checks  instead it had echoes  of what happened after the 737 max  crashes  this action by the faa was a sign  of its increasing scrutiny  when it came to overseeing the  manufacturing process of the dreamliner  but later that month boeing resumed  dreamliner deliveries  taking what looked like the first step  toward moving past these issues  in april the company's ceo david calhoun  told investors the 787 delivery pause  was an example of the company trying to  solve its manufacturing issues  we've taken actions over this really  hard actions over the course this course  of this year  to stop things when we see an issue and  get them fixed once and for all  then in late may deliveries were halted  again as the faa requested more  information from boeing about its  proposed solution for catching and  addressing the defects before handing  over the planes to customers  boeing was hoping that it could get faa  approval  for the methodology to inspect using a  mix of physical inspections  and analysis with those discussions  ongoing boeing found the latest defect  around early july  this time on part of the aircraft known  as the forward pressure bulkhead at the  front of the plane  so it's going to complicate the process  that was underway of  going trying to prove to the faa that  its methodology  was sound it's still unclear when the  latest issue will be resolved though  boeing said it could take at least three  weeks to address  what we're doing on the 787 is a  methodical program  reviewing the airplane from tip to tail  in a way that's safe  and compliant for our customers and our  regulators  meanwhile finnish dreamliners are piling  up more than 100 planes await delivery  boeing has slowed production on the  plane and said it now expects to deliver  less than half of its 787  inventory this year the slowdown is  likely to ripple into  boeing's vast supply network and  for some weeks and months it's going to  mean that  boeing's airline customers have to wait  longer to get their new  dreamliners it's unclear whether these  manufacturing issues will matter in the  long run  whether or not there's any long-term  impact  good or bad to boeing's reputation  remains to be seen for now the  dreamliner is still a very popular  aircraft and has an excellent safety  record  [Music]
 

